SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Supreme Court Upholds Right to Vote by Mail
DESCRIPTION:AUGUSTA\, MAINE\n– Absentee voting by mail will continue ju
 st as it has in prior elections\, announced Secretary of State Shenna Bell
 ows today.  The US Supreme Court rejected the US Postal Service and Trump
  Administration’s quest to change the rules for mailed ballots.  States
  including the State of Maine had filed a lawsuit to challenge the Trump E
 xecutive Order and US Postal Service rule that would have dramatically lim
 ited the ability of clerks to mail ballots to voters.  A lower court bloc
 ked the rule on constitutional grounds\, and the US Supreme Court denied t
 he Trump Administration’s attempt to overturn the lower court.\n“This 
 is good news for voters and for democracy.  Voters can rest assured that 
 they have all of the same options for absentee voting including receiving 
 and returning their ballot by mail that they have had in every other elect
 ion\,” said Secretary of State Shenna Bellows.  “We know that many Ma
 iners – particularly seniors and people with disabilities – appreciate
  the convenience of voting from home\, and we are thrilled that our joint 
 legal efforts with states across the country to protect the right to vote 
 by mail have succeeded.”\nVoters can request absentee ballots utilizing 
 the Secretary of State’s online\nabsentee ballot portal\n\, in writing\,
  or by calling their\nmunicipal clerk\ndirectly. Absentee voting in Maine 
 begins October 5\, and voters should start receiving their ballots in the 
 mail in the first week of October. Voters can track the status of their ab
 sentee ballot via Maine’s\nonline absentee ballot tracking portal\n.\n##
 #\nhttps://www.maine.gov/sos/news/us-supreme-court-upholds-right-vote-mail
URL:https://www.maine.gov/sos/news/us-supreme-court-upholds-right-vote-mail
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b5bd58f77b557ce6@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260915T165640Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] National Voter Registration Day Today\, Septe
 mber 15
DESCRIPTION:AUGUSTA\, MAINE\n– Today\, September 15\, is National Voter
  Registration Day\, a nonpartisan civic holiday dedicated to celebrating d
 emocracy and registering voters.\n"Registering to vote is the first step f
 or every citizen to participate in our democracy and have your voice heard
 \," said Secretary of State Shenna Bellows. "We like to say that every day
  in Maine is voter registration day because in our state you can even regi
 ster to vote on Election Day\, but this holiday is an important reminder o
 f the fundamental right to vote and have your voice heard."\nSecretary Bel
 lows will visit voter registration events around the state today to speak 
 with Mainers about the importance of registering to vote. She is available
  to do in interviews upon request.\nTo register to vote in Maine\, voters 
 must be Maine residents\, U.S. citizens\, and at least 16 years old\, thou
 gh only Mainers who will be 18 years old on or before November 3\, 2026 ma
 y vote in the General Election. When registering for the first time\, vote
 rs must provide proof of\nresidency\nand\nidentity\n. Current or former in
 carceration status does not disqualify any Mainer from registering to vote
  or casting their ballot. Incarcerated persons at a correctional facility 
 or county jail may register to vote in the Maine municipality where they e
 stablished residency prior to incarceration.\nMaine does not currently off
 er a voter registration lookup tool\, but voters can contact their town or
  city clerk if they are unsure if they are already registered to vote\, or
  if their information is up-to-date. Contact information for municipal cle
 rks and registrars is available at\nhttps://www.maine.gov/sos/cec/elec/mun
 ic.html\n.\nMaine has same day voter registration\, meaning that Mainers m
 ay register to vote and cast their ballot on the same day\, even Election 
 Day. Online voter registration at\nmaine.gov/vote\nis available through 5 
 pm on October 13 (21 days before Election Day). Registrations done by mail
  need to be received by the municipal clerk of \nhttps://www.maine.gov/sos
 /news/national-voter-registration-day-today-september-15
URL:https://www.maine.gov/sos/news/national-voter-registration-day-today-s
 eptember-15
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
